      "id": 1,
      "text": "Iselin was renamed in 1872 after philanthropist Adrian Iselin, who funded a Victorian train station that spurred growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Iselin,_New_Jersey",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The area once called Perrytown or Uniontown grew after railroads arrived in the 1830s and post‑WWI housing, earning the nickname “Bungalowtown” in the 1920s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Iselin,_New_Jersey",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1990s, an influx of Asian Indian immigrants revitalized Iselin, turning it into a hub for Indian cuisine, sweets, and saris.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iselin%2C_New_Jersey",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Iselin sits 9 miles northeast of New Brunswick and 14 miles southwest of Newark, placing it between two major New Jersey cities.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iselin%2C_New_Jersey",
